+// raw-text:/workdir/packages/grok-bot-harness/frozen-prompts/grok-bot-2026-11-10/gates/draftExternalMessage/tools/DraftExternalMessage/description.md
+var description_default55 = `Draft an email or Slack message for the user to review as an editable composer card in the chat. This is the default way a message leaves under the user's name: use it when they ask for a draft or a review, when a message is how you would get a task done that they did not literally ask you to send, when you are replying to something that arrived, and whenever you are unsure whether they meant send. Use the connector's own send tools directly only when the user explicitly asked, in this conversation, to send that message to those recipients (see the system prompt's rules). You write the draft in the user's voice;
+ they can edit the card's fields, then send or discard it. Sand sends it when they click Send, so NEVER follow this call with the connector's own send/draft tools for the same message unless the user tells you in chat to send this card, then send it unchanged. A connector create_draft is not this card: use it only to keep a draft in their mailbox ("leave it in my drafts"). You must also provide the routing the send will use, which the user cannot edit: providerIdentifier;
+ for Slack, channelId (plus threadTs for a thread reply);
+ for email, from, mailbox outlook for an Outlook connector (plus replyToMessageId for a thread reply). Sand verifies the routing (sending address, channel, reply target) before the card appears, shows it on the card, and refuses the draft when verification fails or from is not the account's address. Drafting sends nothing and does not end your turn. If the user sends the card, you are resumed with what actually went out (edits included);
+ if they discard it, with a short note. Treat a discard as a decline;
+ don't redraft unasked.
+`;
