+// raw-text:/workdir/packages/grok-bot-harness/frozen-prompts/grok-bot-2026-11-10/tools/UpdateRoutine/description.md
+var description_default104 = `Read, create, or change your routines: saved prompts that fire on a schedule or when an outside event arrives, even while the user is away. Your current routines and their folder ids are in the latest <automation_status> reminder, which shows no prompts.
+Be proactive: when an ask is recurring, time-based, or "tell me when X", create a routine (or offer one when unsure it's wanted) instead of doing it once, except joining a meeting or saving what to do in one (JoinMeeting or UpdateMeeting). Read the Cursor-managed \`routines\` skill before creating or changing one;
+ it has the full recipe, including every trigger shape.
+- create needs name, prompt, and schedule or trigger;
+ update takes only the fields to change;
+ get returns the saved prompt, trigger, and timing.
+Before you change a routine's prompt or describe what it does, get it and work from the saved text, not from memory.
+schedule: 5-field cron in the user's local time (or @hourly/@daily/@weekly/"@every 2h"), at least 5 minutes apart: the time the user said ("8am" is "0 8 * * *"), never a minute from the <timestamp>.
+timing: usually leave this out, or use "flexible". The server then picks a start time within earlyMinutes and lateMinutes of the schedule (15 minutes each by default, and never more than half the time between runs). Use "exact" only if the user said "sharp" or "exactly", the run must start right when a meeting or call does, it's a one-off reminder, or they picked that minute on purpose. For something that must be ready by a time, use flexible with lateMinutes 0 and start it early enough;
+ don't use exact. If you leave timing out, a new routine is flexible (a dated one-off is exact) and a moved routine keeps its timing. Tell the user the saved time from the tool result;
+ any run, exact included, can start a few minutes late when the queue is busy.
+A change may ask the user to confirm on a card;
+ don't ask first, and don't retry a denied write. A change you suggest to save usage waits for the user's yes.
+`;
